bout  our society  um  that  people doing this work people who are  willing to give it they're all you know  aren't being supported and don't have  the support that they need to  in so many cases keep going because like  we see in the film with Gregory burning  out  um it just wasn't sustainable for him  and I mean I want to live in a society  where we're helping the people the  helpers we're helping the helpers and  we're  um  where people who are willing to fight  against the system that's so broken are  being supported and they don't have to  choose you know All or Nothing basically  um and it's a I think it's a rare  individual  who could who could make that choice  um so I think it's really a societal  issue and it's again something that I'm  hoping people will will reflect upon and  ask you know is this the way that I want  our world to work  in our country and around the world  right  was so is rapid defense network  um a non-profit organization that that  survives on donations is that what the  issue is  yeah so uh so they are a two-person you  know uh or they were at the start of the  film a kind of a two-person team  um and they realized that there was this  huge unmet need that people uh you know  uh working in some of the bigger  organizations the kind of national you  know ones that we've all heard of uh  there's you know with any kind of big  bureaucracy there's red tape and there's  rules I know you can't do this and you  can't do that and they were in this  situation where they were like we want  to do everything we possibly can we're  gonna try and pull rabbits out of hats  because the stakes are so high and so  we're gonna do things in ways that you  know maybe people don't normally do them  and we might break some rules you know  calling in at the side of uh of a  highway and and doing a you know hearing  over the phone but it worked and so that  kind of uh their you know commitment to  kind of doing whatever it takes and  trying new strategies and being willing  to lose or being willing to you know  humiliate themselves in front of a judge  because you know there are they're not  winning was a kind of was what they felt  was what they were willing to do because  they wanted to help families however  they could and so  they basically you know they had no  funding they had no offices they had no  staff  um and they were kind of drinking from  like a fire hose of you know the number  of people who needed their help because  they started winning these cases and  they were becoming really successful  they were changing the way that people  were using the law to fight against  illegal uh deportation and detention and  so they were looking for funding they  were applying they were kind of doing  everything they could they were asking  for for donations and that was kind of  you know what they were supporting  themselves on I think they were you know  supporting themselves on their own  personal savings and you know Gregory  was having to sell his apartment and you  know uh and and at a certain point it  just you know it couldn't continue after  you know a couple of years of of just  trying to do that and that's I think  what was so kind of sad to us because  they were so successful and they were  doing things in this way that was doing  such good work and there's just such  little funding and support for these  communities and the people supporting  these communities uh it's so under  resource and they're all kind of uh you  know the organizations in these fields  are you know they're all fighting for  this very small allocation of funding  from you know Public Funding and donors  and so so ultimately  um  you know it just it couldn't keep going  and that you know that's such a a sad I  mean Sarah obviously continued it on but  I think kind of as a as a team also the  emotional toll that it took on Gregory I  mean that was something I think his one  of his main reasons like uh you know the  it takes a really singular uh type of  person you know as a psychiatrist too I  experienced burnout  um working in you know settings with  children uh when you  when you can't help people in the way  that you dream of helping them when you  have to be the container for so much  suffering and uh Injustice uh it's it's  really you know it's it's worked for the  people living the trauma uh but it's  also uh a challenge for those trying to  support and so finding that way to like  as a you know group if if you're willing  to be a pro bono lawyer you know after  with all of your debt from law school  and whatever it is you're willing to go  out there and and and work for you know  no money and and uh and put in those  hours and to at least have the basic you  know covering their costs covering their  office space getting them you know a  student that can support them just those  those basic things I think uh you know  are things that would be  would serve so many families if if we  could kind of find a way of prioritizing  um some of that support  so rapid defense network still exists  um is there a way for just like regular  people to support them do they take like  private donations and  do they have like a website and  yeah yeah  so they basically separated you know  ways Sarah one of the lawyers continued  on and then literally just like a month  or two ago I don't actually know the  exact details but she merged uh her kind  of organization of one  um with uh RFK human rights and has now  become their kind of you director of U.S  litigation and so she has kind of Taken  rapid defense network the clients that  she's serving the work that they've been  doing and kind of integrated it under  this umbrella  um organization where  um where she's kind of continuing to do  the same work and really kind of expand  take those take that work and expand it  um so I think it's like super exciting  so certainly you know supporting her  work at RFK human rights is an option  and then uh  uh and then there are lots of you know  there are other you know immigrant you  know support uh organizations all over  the country because obviously  um these challenges are facing  communities all over the country so  um but yeah but so to support Sarah and  like in her work and the clients that  she's working with  um you can do that directly through uh  RFK human rights  great  so did Gregory stay in this work or did  he start like a Basset Hound Rescue  his dogs his dogs were like you should  ask that he yeah he's not currently  practicing but he is he is living  Upstate with his dogs and  um I think other other pets but we you  know as such fans of his hope that he'll  find a way back into helping people  again one day  well thanks so much to the two of you  for coming in and talking to us about  about your film today  um I wish you the best of success do you  think that the two of you will keep  making films  um in this area of documentary in  immigration reform  I think we're definitely going to keep  working together and I think you know we  both kind of have worked in this in this  field before I think almost kind of  I think there's a few different ideas  almost macroscoping up even further to  the issue of kind of  the climate influence uh you know is  also something we've been kind of  talking about so I think I think we both  feel committed to to continuing to work  on kind of things that we feel are  Urgent problems uh that we want to  somehow uh Castle light on shine a light  on  um kind of  overcome the sense of hopelessness and  helplessness that we felt by finding  people who kind of can hopefully restore  some hope and a pathway of like you know  what can be done and like not giving up  and uh that I think is that's the goal  for me is just kind of and you know for  work going forward is like how can we  continue to do things that help uh  kind of uh  move things in the right direction  well great absolutely and thank you so  much for having us Jen it's been so  lovely to chat with you thank you I'm so  glad that I got the opportunity to see  the film and talk with you it's  definitely a really interesting  conversation and inspiring film  thank you thank you thanks  [Music]


